fix(memory): take the copy a failed unlink left under a name nobody can spell

Round 20 review, Grok 4.6: putting the entry back under its own name
leaves the reserved name pointing at the same inode when the drop cannot
be retried, and the retry that follows removes the visible name and only
that one. The manifest row is then retired for a file still in the user
vault under a name no listing shows -- a withdrawal reporting completion
over a note that is still there. The same happens when the put-back never
took the original name at all: the path holds nothing, the absence is
durable, and the row drains.

A withdrawal now sweeps the reserved entries in the inbox whose whole
bytes are exactly the ones it was already entitled to delete, through the
same detach-and-verify path every other removal here takes, and keeps the
row for any it could not remove. A reserved entry it cannot name -- a
half-written note another writer is staging, a file this package never
made -- is left exactly where it is: the bytes are what authorise a
removal, and a row that never named any authorises nothing.

Co-authored-by: Copilot <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
